        heading: 'Setting Type Well',
        body: `Beyond choosing typefaces, setting type well — the details of how type is arranged — is the craft that distinguishes professional typography. Understanding the details of setting type is essential to typography that reads well and looks polished.

The size of type — appropriate sizes for different purposes — affects both readability and hierarchy. Type size must be appropriate — large enough to read comfortably for body text, sized appropriately for the purpose (larger for headlines, smaller for captions). Size affects readability (too small is hard to read) and hierarchy (size differences distinguish elements). Setting appropriate type sizes is fundamental to setting type well. Understanding the importance of type size is foundational to setting type. Appropriate type size affects readability and hierarchy.

Line spacing (leading) — the space between lines — significantly affects readability. The space between lines of type (leading) significantly affects readability — too little spacing makes lines cramped and hard to read, while appropriate spacing gives the text room and makes it comfortable to read. Setting appropriate line spacing — adequate space between lines — is a key detail of setting type well. Understanding the importance of line spacing is part of setting type. Line spacing (leading) significantly affects readability.

Line length (measure) — the length of lines of text — affects readability. The length of lines of text (measure) affects readability — lines too long are hard to read (the eye struggling to track to the next line), lines too short are choppy. An appropriate line length — in the comfortable range — supports readability. Setting appropriate line length is a detail of setting type well. Understanding the importance of line length is part of setting type. Line length (measure) affects readability.

Letter spacing (tracking and kerning) — the space between letters — affects the appearance and readability of type. The space between letters — overall (tracking) and between specific pairs (kerning) — affects the appearance and readability of type. Appropriate letter spacing (not too tight or loose overall, with problematic letter pairs kerned) makes type look polished and read well, while poor letter spacing makes type look amateur or read poorly. Setting appropriate letter spacing is a detail that distinguishes professional typography. Understanding the importance of letter spacing is part of setting type. Letter spacing (tracking and kerning) affects appearance and readability.

The alignment and other details — alignment, paragraph treatment, and other typographic details — complete setting type well. Other details — alignment (how text is aligned, with left alignment usual for readability), paragraph treatment (how paragraphs are separated and indented), and the many other typographic details — complete the craft of setting type well. Attention to these details — the alignment, the paragraph treatment, the consistency, the refinement of all the small choices — distinguishes professional typography. Understanding that the details complete setting type well underscores the craft of typography. Alignment and other typographic details complete the craft of setting type well.`
